 of like that  although he'd reject some of the  underground man's premises think about  Thomas Aquinas right the intellect knows  reality in certain ways it knows it  under the aspect of the true but the  will knows reality in a certain respect  even more directly even though it  doesn't necessarily cog NIH's it and  does so through practicality right the  realm of practical reasoning in which  desires and pursuit are analogous to  affirmation all right and version and  going away are analogous but not exactly  the same as denial the will grasps the  good and grasps reality as such which  even actually opens up a realm for our  emotions to insert in a certain way be a  thing connector not a thing a mode  connecting us to life more directly  now of course Thomas is more of a  rationalist than this underground man  he's got a great deal of confidence in  reason but we can see that this is a  part of the philosophical tradition we  don't have to go to somebody like Hume  saying reason can only be the slave of  the passions to recognize that the realm  of desire may in fact be much wider than  the realm of rash  allottee according to the underground  man he brings up another really  important set of points as well and we  touched on this in talking about desire  being more encompassing than reason or  rationality desire always possesses a  certain priority not only a practical  one but we could say an ontological one  one of the very core of human being over  rationality he tells us that desire can  if it wishes agree with reason that's up  to desire we often think that that  reason in some way or we portray reason  as in some way being able to coerce and  impose itself upon desire a must desire  actually decides to go along with it you  know sort of like indulging ones you  know eccentric uncle and saying oh yeah  sure let's go take a walk with you let's  see where we're going rationality  if desire doesn't choose to do that then  reason can talk and blather all at once  but it's really just talking to itself  desire is already thinking of the stuff  that it wants and not paying any  attention to what reason was a great  emblem of this could be thinking about  the typical college classroom where we  know from you know psychological  experiments that many of the students  are not actually paying attention the  entire time though they an instructor  might believe that they they are and  it's up to the instructor to somehow  reach the students and sometimes the  students won't be reached there are some  days where nobody's paying any attention  whatsoever because their minds around  something else so if we if desire does  go along with reads and it does so  because it chooses to or desires to or  wishes to write what else well what's  the normal case for desire and and  reason he says in reality desire usually  stubbornly disagrees with reason the  underground man goes further than most  people will and actually against many of  them in saying  and let me tell you this too is useful  and praiseworthy typically in philosophy  we look at desire and reason and their  conflict as if you know if there is a  disagreement we should always be on the  side of reason desire needs to shape up  and and quit you know being so obstinate  about these sorts of things and if it  does then it's you know it's good and if  it doesn't then it's bad and Dostoevsky  is willing to say well no maybe  sometimes right is on the side of desire  and not rationality the last thing and  this leads us into other discussions is  that perhaps it can be a desire for us  that we get to desire that we get to  follow our desires even if they are  irrational as he says a little bit  earlier talking about the stone wall and  saying that it's sort of the  metaphysical equivalent of people saying  two plus two equals four he says yes I  totally recognize that the rational part  of me grasped that that doesn't mean  that I have to accept it  I accept of course that the wall is  there I don't accept that I have some  sort of role in creating it or that I'm  responsible for it or that I even have  to follow the rule that's involved in  the wall I can smash my head into it if  I wish to perhaps it is true that we  actually have a desire to be able to  desire and you might call this following  Harry Frankfurt a second-order desire if  you like Dostoevsky is talking about  this a long time before that well over a  hundred years and this would be a desire  to actually get to decide things for  ourselves so as you can see desire has a  great priority over rationality for the  underground man and arguably for  Dostoevsky himself and this is in  opposition to much of the long-standing  tradition of philosophy  you
 

